% ============================================================================
% biome-type — four named biomes and what actually defines each
% (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A biome is a large-scale climate zone, and each one is defined by a
% different limiting condition: a desert by how little rain falls, a
% forest by tree cover, a grassland by open grass-dominated terrain, a
% tundra by extreme cold. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? biome_type(desert, $D)
%       % dry_areas_where_rainfall_is_less_than_50_centimeters_20_inches_per_year
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `biome_type(biome, description)` carrying the source citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on a biome-related term that is not one of these
% four. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the
% biome it names:
%
%     ? biome_type($B, dominated_by_trees_and_cover_about_one_third_of_the_earth)
%       % forest
%
% The four biomes, as a little truth table:
%
%     biome        description                                                                         quote (verbatim)
%     -----------  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     desert        dry_areas_where_rainfall_is_less_than_50_centimeters_20_inches_per_year               "Deserts are dry areas where rainfall is less than 50 centimeters (20 inches) per year."
%     forest        dominated_by_trees_and_cover_about_one_third_of_the_earth                             "Forests are dominated by trees, and cover about one-third of the Earth."
%     grassland     open_regions_dominated_by_grass_with_a_warm_dry_climate                                "Grasslands are open regions that are dominated by grass and have a warm, dry climate."
%     tundra        has_extremely_inhospitable_conditions_with_the_lowest_measured_temperatures            "A tundra has extremely inhospitable conditions, with the lowest measured temperatures of any of the five major biomes."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from National Geographic Education's "The
% Five Major Types of Biomes" article, which introduces each of these
% four biomes under its own subheading, each with a complete defining
% sentence. WebFetch-verified before writing (twice — the second pass
% specifically pulled the full surrounding paragraph for each candidate
% sentence to confirm it stands alone grammatically and isn't qualified
% or contradicted by an immediately adjacent sentence). `trust consensus`
% — National Geographic Education is a reputable secondary reference,
% the same tier already used for `map-type.adj`'s Geology.com citation
% and `figurative-language-type.adj`'s Grammarly citation. A biome that
% is NOT one of these four — "aquatic", the source's fifth major biome,
% but one whose own section opens by deferring to its freshwater and
% marine sub-categories ("Aquatic biomes include both freshwater and
% marine biomes...") rather than stating a single, complete defining
% sentence the way the four tabled here do — is deliberately NOT a row,
% so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a description.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table biome_type {
    columns biome, description

    row (desert, dry_areas_where_rainfall_is_less_than_50_centimeters_20_inches_per_year)
    row (forest, dominated_by_trees_and_cover_about_one_third_of_the_earth)
    row (grassland, open_regions_dominated_by_grass_with_a_warm_dry_climate)
    row (tundra, has_extremely_inhospitable_conditions_with_the_lowest_measured_temperatures)

    source "Deserts are dry areas where rainfall is less than 50 centimeters (20 inches) per year."
    locator "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/five-major-types-biomes/"
    trust consensus
}
